upd("n622", append="[Checked in Badius 1518 via SCTA: *Quodl.* VIII q. 9 counts eleven modes, ending ‘Vndecimus modus est eius quod nec ex se nec ab alio est possibile esse’ (the *res a reor reris*, a being of imagination). Henry’s second mode (‘ex se formaliter ens: sed … ab alio’) is subdivided ‘tripliciter … principiatiue … causatiue … dependenter’. Gorris counts these three as his modes 2–4. Henry’s fifth mode is ‘ex se possibile esse: sed non possibile non esse, sed ab alio causatiue … necesse esse’, which Henry says implies contradiction. His sixth is ‘ex se possibile esse: sed ab alio causatiue necessario habet esse’ (Avicenna’s position). Gorris’s fifth corresponds to Henry’s sixth. His garbled sixth (*et non est ab alio tamen necesse esse*) is most likely a corrupt summary of Henry’s fifth (*non possibile non esse*). The English translates the text as printed. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n623", append="[Checked: purely textual, with no external source; it is confirmed against B as stated. Verified as a doublet.]", status="verified")
upd("n625", append="[Checked in Badius 1518 via SCTA: the last question of *Quodl.* VI on SCTA is q. 33, ‘Utrum elementa sint distincta per corpora caelestia’. It discusses whether the heavens have *necesse esse* of themselves, with Averroes, *De substantia orbis*. That fits Gorris’s description, but the modes as listed there were not compared one by one.]", status="probable")
